<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Windows Installer package for Polylinker.

  WHY THIS FILE LIVES IN tools/installer/ AND NOT A DIRECTORY OF ITS OWN.
  `tools/ci.ps1` walks every file under tools/installer/ and fails the gate if
  any of them mentions a network, service or scheduled-task facility. That scan
  was written for Install-Polylinker.ps1, and putting the .wxs anywhere else
  would have quietly exempted the installer that people will actually
  double-click from the refusals the project makes in its own documentation.
  It is here so it is scanned. An MSI has elements for all three — ServiceInstall,
  RemoteFile, and a bundle that downloads its own payload — and this package
  uses none of them.

  WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN: a list of files.
  docs/RELEASING.md argued against a compiled installer on exactly that ground —
  "every compiled installer has a second file list", which has already drifted
  twice in this project's short history. So the payload is not written here. It
  is GENERATED by tools/build-msi.ps1 from dist/SHA256SUMS.txt, the same manifest
  Install-Polylinker.ps1 reads and the same one check-archive.ps1 verifies, and a
  gate step asserts the generated component set equals the manifest exactly.
  There is still one file list; the MSI is a second reader of it, not a second
  copy of it.

  SCOPE. perUserOrMachine, defaulting to per-user, because the readers this is
  for are frequently on institutional machines where they are not
  administrators. That was the entire reason Install-Polylinker.ps1 was per-user,
  and an MSI that forced elevation would take that away. It also avoids the
  "Unknown Publisher" elevation prompt on the default path, which matters
  because nothing here is signed.

  ASSOCIATIONS ARE ADDITIVE. WiX's <Extension> element writes the extension's
  default value, which takes the file type away from whatever the reader already
  has — SnapGene owns .dna on a lot of these machines. This package therefore
  writes OpenWithProgids entries by hand instead, exactly as
  Install-Polylinker.ps1 does, so Polylinker joins the "Open with" list without
  displacing anyone. See the comment on C_Associations.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">

  <!--
    Version is passed in by tools/build-msi.ps1 with -d Version=..., read from
    the workspace Cargo.toml. It is NOT written here. Cargo.toml is the single
    source for the version and three other places already re-read it rather than
    restating it; a literal here would be the first copy nothing checks.
  -->
  <?ifndef Version ?>
  <?error Version was not supplied. Build through tools/build-msi.ps1, which reads it from Cargo.toml. ?>
  <?endif?>

  <Package
      Name="Polylinker"
      Manufacturer="The Polylinker contributors"
      Version="$(Version)"
      UpgradeCode="78205503-D26C-4A6B-82DE-E0F834220A6D"
      Scope="perUserOrMachine"
      Compressed="yes"
      InstallerVersion="500"
      Language="1033"
      Codepage="1252">

    <!--
      Manufacturer is "The Polylinker contributors" because that is what the
      version resource in every shipped .exe already says (bins/winres.rs,
      CompanyName). Add/Remove Programs derives its Publisher column from this,
      so the alternative was an installer whose publisher disagreed with the
      binaries it installed. Install-Polylinker.ps1 writes Publisher=Polylinker;
      that is the one the MSI supersedes, and README-WINDOWS.txt says so.

      UpgradeCode is permanent. It identifies "Polylinker" to Windows across
      every version that will ever ship, and changing it would make a new
      release install alongside the old one instead of replacing it. Minted
      2026-08-05, asserted by a gate step so it cannot be edited by accident.
    -->

    <MajorUpgrade
        AllowDowngrades="no"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Polylinker is already installed. Uninstall it from Settings &gt; Apps before installing this one."
        Schedule="afterInstallValidate" />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />

    <!--
      No network, no auto-updater, no service, no scheduled task, no telemetry.
      The same refusals Install-Polylinker.ps1 makes, and the reason this file
      sits in a directory that is scanned for them: the gate step "the installer
      contacts nothing" in tools/ci.ps1 reads every file in this directory and
      fails if it finds any of twelve download or scheduling facilities. The
      twelve are named there and deliberately not here, because that scan does
      not strip comments, so a file that listed them would fail the very check
      it was describing.

      This read "no updater" until 2026-08-06, and the word that changed carries
      the whole distinction. Polylinker gained an update check that day: `pl
      update`, a verb somebody types, and a box in the editor's Help menu that
      ships unticked. Neither one is installed BY this package or by anything in
      it. They are code inside pl.exe and polylinker.exe, which this package
      copies and does not modify. What this MSI still puts on the machine is
      files, a shortcut, an Add/Remove Programs entry and (opt-in) file
      associations; nothing it installs ever runs unless a person starts it.
      See docs/RELEASING.md, "There is no auto-updater, on purpose".

      (Two hyphens in a row would make this file invalid XML, which is why
      nothing here is punctuated with them.)
    -->

    <Icon Id="PolylinkerIcon" SourceFile="!(bindpath.payload)polylinker.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="PolylinkerIcon" />
    <Property Id="ARPNOREPAIR" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="ARPHELPLINK" Value="https://github.com/liorlobel/polylinker" />
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/liorlobel/polylinker" />

    <!--
      Default to a per-user install: "for me" preselected, which is the path
      that needs no administrator and raises no elevation prompt.
      WixUI_Advanced shows the "for me" / "for everyone" choice.

      ALLUSERS and MSIINSTALLPERUSER are NOT authored here. Scope="perUserOrMachine"
      above makes the compiler emit them itself (ALLUSERS=2, MSIINSTALLPERUSER=1),
      and restating them is a duplicate-symbol error that fails the link. That is
      the standard v3-to-v5 migration trap: v3 authored ALLUSERS by hand and v5
      forbids it.
    -->
    <Property Id="WixAppFolder" Value="WixPerUserFolder" />
    <Property Id="ApplicationFolderName" Value="Polylinker" />

    <!--
      No InstallDirectory attribute. It would add a WIXUI_INSTALLDIR property,
      and the WixUI_Advanced wixlib already defines one — a duplicate symbol,
      again at link time. This dialog set is parameterised by NAMING the install
      directory APPLICATIONFOLDER, which is what the StandardDirectory block
      below does.
    -->
    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_Advanced" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="!(bindpath.msi)LICENSE.rtf" />

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="Polylinker" Level="1" AllowAbsent="no"
             Description="The editor, the command line, the MCP server, the Python module and the licence texts.">
      <!-- Generated by tools/build-msi.ps1 from dist/SHA256SUMS.txt. -->
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="PayloadComponents" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_Shortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_AppPaths" />
    </Feature>

    <Feature Id="PathEntry" Title="Add pl to PATH" Level="1" AllowAbsent="yes"
             Description="Put the install directory on PATH so 'pl' works in a new terminal. Uncheck to leave PATH alone.">
      <!--
        BOTH PATH components belong to this feature. Their Conditions decide
        which one actually installs; the feature only decides whether the reader
        wanted PATH touched at all. Referencing one of them would silently give
        the other scope no PATH entry while still showing the checkbox.
      -->
      <ComponentRef Id="C_Path" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_PathUser" />
    </Feature>

    <Feature Id="FileAssociations" Title="Open sequence files with Polylinker" Level="1" AllowAbsent="yes"
             Description="Add Polylinker to the 'Open with' list for GenBank, FASTA, SnapGene and trace files. It does not take any file type away from a program you already use.">
      <ComponentRef Id="C_ProgIds" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_Associations" />
    </Feature>

    <!--
      THE DIRECTORY MUST BE CALLED APPLICATIONFOLDER, and the name is the whole
      mechanism rather than a label.
      WixUI_Advanced's WixSetPerUserFolder and WixSetPerMachineFolder custom
      actions set a property of exactly that name — to
      [LocalAppDataFolder]Programs\[ApplicationFolderName] when the reader picks
      "for me", and to [ProgramFiles6432Folder][ApplicationFolderName] when they
      pick "for everyone". A directory called anything else is never redirected,
      so the parent below is only the per-machine default.

      The first draft of this file called it INSTALLFOLDER, which meant the
      DEFAULT per-user install would have tried to write to C:\Program Files and
      failed with error 1303 for any reader without administrator rights — the
      exact reader the per-user default exists for. Nothing in a build would have
      caught that; it needed a per-user install to be attempted.
    -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles6432Folder">
      <Directory Id="APPLICATIONFOLDER" Name="Polylinker" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder" />

    <!--
      The Start Menu shortcut. One loose shortcut, not a folder containing one
      shortcut — matching what Install-Polylinker.ps1 creates, and what a
      single-application product should do.

      The keypath is a registry value rather than the shortcut itself because a
      per-user component needs an HKCU keypath for Windows Installer to track
      it. HKMU resolves to HKCU or HKLM according to the scope the reader chose.
    -->
    <Component Id="C_Shortcut" Guid="CC365DB2-7B0D-4AF4-ABAE-31A2013286AA" Directory="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Shortcut Id="StartMenuShortcut"
                Name="Polylinker"
                Description="Offline plasmid editor"
                Target="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe"
                WorkingDirectory="APPLICATIONFOLDER"
                Icon="PolylinkerIcon" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Polylinker" Name="StartMenu" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      App Paths, so Win+R "polylinker" works without touching PATH. This is the
      long-standing Windows mechanism for exactly that and costs one key.
    -->
    <Component Id="C_AppPaths" Guid="A4F9592A-468C-4F70-8DEB-2994F8B5E273" Directory="APPLICATIONFOLDER">
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\App Paths\polylinker.exe"
                     Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\App Paths\polylinker.exe"
                     Name="Path" Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]" Type="string" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      PATH, as TWO components, because Environment/@System cannot be decided at
      run time.
      It is a yes/no attribute resolved while the package is being compiled: it
      chooses whether the row's Name gets the '*' prefix that means "system
      variable" in the MSI Environment table. Writing System="[ALLUSERS]" is not
      a dynamic version of that, it is a compile error — the only legal values
      are the literals. So the choice is made by shipping both rows and letting
      exactly one of them install.

      Windows Installer removes whichever one installed on uninstall by itself,
      which is the part the PowerShell installer had to hand-roll and get right.
    -->
    <Component Id="C_Path" Guid="6005941F-7F67-4349-B62E-86AC5CD8935F" Directory="APPLICATIONFOLDER"
               Condition="ALLUSERS=1">
      <Environment Id="PathEntryMachine" Name="PATH" Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]" Action="set" Part="last"
                   System="yes" Permanent="no" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Polylinker" Name="PathEntry" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <Component Id="C_PathUser" Guid="4B850F96-6DDA-4F77-A7FC-A4117E8AB3FD" Directory="APPLICATIONFOLDER"
               Condition="NOT ALLUSERS=1">
      <Environment Id="PathEntryUser" Name="PATH" Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]" Action="set" Part="last"
                   System="no" Permanent="no" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Polylinker" Name="PathEntryUser" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      THE PROGIDS.

      One ProgID per kind of thing, not one per extension, so the "Open with"
      list shows a meaningful name. The command is `polylinker.exe "%1"`.

      Polylinker.Project is registered but NOT associated with .plproj below.
      The GUI decides a file's format by CONTENT, through pl_fileio, and no
      crate under crates/ knows the .plproj format at all — it is a bench file
      read by bins/pl-gui/src/session.rs, reached from a menu. Double-clicking
      one therefore reaches load() -> load_as() -> a content sniff that does not
      recognise it. Install-Polylinker.ps1 takes the DEFAULT handler for .plproj,
      which is the one extension where double-clicking cannot work; this package
      does not repeat that. See README-WINDOWS.txt.
    -->
    <Component Id="C_ProgIds" Guid="67AF247A-CD30-4B79-B614-D1CE39843457" Directory="APPLICATIONFOLDER">
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Sequence" Value="Nucleotide sequence" Type="string" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Sequence\DefaultIcon" Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe,0" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Sequence\shell\open\command" Value="&quot;[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe&quot; &quot;%1&quot;" Type="string" />

      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Trace" Value="Sanger trace" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Trace\DefaultIcon" Value="[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe,0" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\Polylinker.Trace\shell\open\command" Value="&quot;[APPLICATIONFOLDER]polylinker.exe&quot; &quot;%1&quot;" Type="string" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      THE ASSOCIATIONS, ADDITIVELY.

      Each extension gets an OpenWithProgids entry and nothing else. The
      extension's own default value is never written, so whatever already owns
      .dna or .gb on this machine keeps owning it and Polylinker simply appears
      in "Open with". This is why <Extension> is not used: it writes the default.

      The eight extensions are the ones Install-Polylinker.ps1 associates, minus
      .plproj, for the reason given above.
    -->
    <Component Id="C_Associations" Guid="5E49D9DA-04D4-46F1-9050-437112E84EE3" Directory="APPLICATIONFOLDER">
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Polylinker" Name="Associations" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />

      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.dna\OpenWithProgids"     Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.gb\OpenWithProgids"      Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.gbk\OpenWithProgids"     Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.genbank\OpenWithProgids" Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.fasta\OpenWithProgids"   Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.fa\OpenWithProgids"      Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.fna\OpenWithProgids"     Name="Polylinker.Sequence" Value="" Type="string" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Classes\.ab1\OpenWithProgids"     Name="Polylinker.Trace"    Value="" Type="string" />
    </Component>

  </Package>
</Wix>
